#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md
msgid "// print_value(\"hello\"); // ❌ Mismatched types!\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md
msgid ""
"If you are coming from a dynamic language like Python, the concept of "
"polymorphism may be new to you because in dynamic languages all functions "
"are implicitly polymorphic. But in Rust, functions only accept arguments of "
"the exact types they expect, and we must use a mechanism like generics to "
"support polymorphism."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md
msgid ""
"Static typing is a powerful tool that allows the compiler to enforce correct "
"usage of our APIs: If your function needs to be given an `i32` in order to "
"function correctly, the compiler won't allow a user of that function to pass "
"in a string."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md
msgid ""
"But static typing is restrictive in cases where we want to write code that "
"is flexible in its handling of types. In the example on this slide, we might "
"want our `print_value` function to be able to print both numbers and "
"strings, but as written it can only accept `i32` values."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md
msgid ""
"Edit `print_value` to make it generic using `Display` to print different "
"kinds of values:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid "In Rust we have 3 different mechanisms for writing polymorphic code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"**Generics** - Static polymorphism where code abstracts over types but the "
"types are fully known at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"**Enums** - Dynamic polymorphism where a fixed set of known types are "
"selected between at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"**`dyn`** - Dynamic polymorphism where any type meeting a particular trait "
"interface can be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"When discussing polymorphism in Rust, it's helpful to differentiate between "
"**static** polymorphism and **dynamic** polymorphism."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"**static polymorphism** is when we abstract over types, but the type "
"information is fully known by the compiler. This allows us to reuse code in "
"different type contexts without needing to add any runtime overhead, and we "
"have access to the full set of features that traits expose. This is "
"accomplished with **generics** in Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"**dynamic polymorphism** is when we need to select between different types "
"at runtime, and we don't know at compile time which specific type will be "
"used."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"When we have a fixed set of known types to choose from, we can use **enums** "
"to track at runtime which one we have."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/polymorphism-kinds.md
msgid ""
"When we don't know ahead of time which types may be used, e.g. if downstream "
"users may introduce new types that we don't know about, then we use "
"**`dyn`** to allow extensibility."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md
msgid ""
"Generics are used when we want to abstract over types, but we expect the "
"users of our code to know the concrete types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md
msgid ""
"Generics are our mechanism for **static polymorphism**, which is "
"polymorphism where the types are fully known at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md
msgid ""
"One example of this is `Vec`, which is generic over the type of element it "
"stores. `Vec` itself is polymorphic: It's written in such a way that it "
"doesn't know what type of element will be stored in it. But in order to use "
"a `Vec`, you must specify a concrete type to use for the element."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md
msgid ""
"Generics are a mechanism for **code reuse**: You have some common logic that "
"is fundamentally the same regardless of what specific type it handles, and "
"generics give you a way to abstract over those different types **without "
"duplicating logic**."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md
msgid ""
"Note that there's no dynamism: The types must be fully known at compile "
"time, there's no way to select a type for `Vec`'s element at runtime. This "
"means that generics are not an option when we need **runtime polymorphism**. "
"Later we will look at two mechanisms for doing dynamic polymorphism: Enums "
"and `dyn`."
msgstr ""

